To access the fourth element of a linked list in Java, you must traverse the list starting from the head node and move forward three times (since the first node is index 0).

Here’s a simple example using a custom `Node` class and a `LinkedList` class:

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

public class LinkedList {
    private Node head;

    // Method to get the fourth element (1-based: 4th node)
    public int getFourthElement() {
        Node current = head;
        int count = 0;

        // Traverse until we reach the 4th node or end of list
        while (current != null && count < 4) {
            current = current.next;
            count++;
        }

        // If count == 4, we successfully reached the 4th node
        if (count == 4) {
            return current.data;
        } else {
            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("List has fewer than 4 elements.");
        }
    }

    // Helper method to add nodes for testing
    public void add(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            Node current = head;
            while (current.next != null) {
                current = current.next;
            }
            current.next = newNode;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList list = new LinkedList();
        list.add(10);
        list.add(20);
        list.add(30);
        list.add(40);
        list.add(50);

        try {
            int fourth = list.getFourthElement(); // Should be 40
            System.out.println("Fourth element: " + fourth);
        } catch (IndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Points:
- Linked lists don’t support random access like arrays, so you must traverse sequentially.
- The fourth element corresponds to index 3 in 0-based indexing, but if you mean the 4th node
